# The Oceans: Relief, Salinity, and Temperature

> Oceans hold about 97% of Earth's water and hide most of its relief
> beneath a lid of blue. This lesson tours the ocean floor and explains
> the salt and heat that govern how ocean water behaves.

## 1. The Water of the Planet

Solar evaporation, vapour transport, precipitation, and runoff endlessly
recycle water between ocean, air, and land — the **hydrological cycle**.

| Term | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ocean | A vast, connected body of salt water | The Pacific Ocean |
| Sea | Smaller, partly land-locked salt water | The Arabian Sea |
| Bay | Wide coastal recess, land on three sides | Bay of Bengal |
| Gulf | Deep recess with a narrow mouth | Gulf of Mexico |
| Strait | Narrow water joining two larger bodies | Palk Strait |
| Isthmus | Narrow land joining two larger landmasses | Isthmus of Panama |

## 2. Relief of the Ocean Floor

@diagram tree id="ocean-floor-relief" layout="tree" aria="Ocean floor features from shelf to trench"
  "Ocean floor" -> "Continental shelf"
  "Ocean floor" -> "Continental slope"
  "Ocean floor" -> "Abyssal plains"
  "Ocean floor" -> "Mid-ocean ridges"
  "Ocean floor" -> Trenches
@/diagram

| Feature | Depth or extent | Significance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Continental shelf | To about 200 m | Less than a tenth of the ocean floor; the world's richest fisheries and offshore oil |
| Continental slope | 200 m to about 2,000–3,000 m | Steep descent to the deep sea |
| Abyssal plains | 3,000–6,000 m | Flattest, most extensive stretches of the globe, sediments settling |
| Mid-ocean ridges | Rising from the plains | World's longest mountain chain (~65,000 km); plates diverge along its rift |
| Seamounts and guyots | Isolated volcanic peaks | Guyots are flat-topped, wave-planed seamounts |
| Ocean trenches | Below 6,000 m | Mariana Trench's Challenger Deep, ~11 km — the planet's deepest point |

[important title="Where the plates meet"]
Trenches mark subduction zones where one plate dives beneath another;
the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench would swallow Mount Everest
with more than 2 km to spare.
[/important]

## 3. Salinity

[definition]
**Salinity** is the salt content of water, expressed in grams per
kilogram (practical salinity units); the world-ocean average is about
**35**.
[/definition]

| Factor | Effect on salinity |
| --- | --- |
| Evaporation | Raises it |
| Precipitation and river influx | Lower it |
| Freezing of sea ice | Raises it (salt is excluded from ice) |

@chart bar title="Salinity of selected waters (practical salinity units)"
labels:
Baltic Sea
World average
Red Sea

data:
8
35
41
@/chart

The **Baltic Sea** reads only about 7–10 because rivers dilute it; the
**Red Sea**, evaporating fiercely with no rivers, reaches about 41;
and the land-locked **Dead Sea**, at roughly 330, is so dense that
swimmers float like corks. Latitudinally, salinity peaks near 20–30°
north and south, where evaporation outruns rainfall.

## 4. Temperature of Ocean Water

| Layer or zone | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| Surface (mixed) layer | Warmest; stirred by winds; tropical waters reach 25–30°C |
| Thermocline | Rapid fall of temperature with depth |
| Deep water | Cold and stable, near 0–2°C at the floor |

Surface temperature falls poleward from about 25–30°C at the equator to
near freezing at the polar margins. Ocean water warms and cools far
more slowly than land, because water has a high heat capacity — which
is why coastal cities enjoy mild, equable climates.

[tip]
Think of the ocean as a three-layer cake: a warm lid, a thermocline
divider, and a vast cold base.
[/tip]

## 5. Wealth of the Oceans

- **Food** — fisheries cluster over the nutrient-rich shelves and
  upwelling zones.
- **Minerals** — oil and gas from shelves; polymetallic nodules on
  abyssal plains.
- **Energy** — tidal, wave, and ocean-thermal prospects.
- **Trade** — most of world trade by weight still travels by sea.

## 6. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Deepest point of the oceans? | Challenger Deep, Mariana Trench (~11 km) |
| Longest mountain chain? | The mid-ocean ridge system (~65,000 km) |
| Average salinity? | About 35 |
| Least saline sea? | The Baltic (about 7–10) |
| Hypersaline example? | The Dead Sea (about 330) |
| Rapid-cooling depth zone? | The thermocline |
| Richest fisheries lie over? | The continental shelf |
